  "early_life": {
    "en": "Born in New Haven, Connecticut, Gibbs was the son of a Yale professor. He studied at Yale and earned one of the first PhD degrees granted in the United States, then spent three years studying in Europe before returning to Yale.",
    "es": "",
    "pt": "",
    "fr": "",
    "de": "",
    "zh": "",
    "hi": "",
    "ar": "",
    "id": "",
    "ja": ""
  },
  "impact": {
    "en": "Gibbs's work on chemical thermodynamics introduced the concept of free energy and the phase rule, which became essential tools in chemistry and materials science. His development of statistical mechanics independently of Boltzmann provided an elegant mathematical framework for the field.",
